meta {
  name: csrf
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{vipa}}/api/v1/auth/csrf
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Run this first, once per Bruno session. It answers 204 and sets the XSRF-TOKEN
  cookie; the collection-level post-response script (server/API/collection.bru) copies
  the value into {{csrfToken}}, which every POST / PUT / PATCH / DELETE in this
  collection sends as the X-XSRF-TOKEN header.

  Since US-006 the server refuses any write arriving without a matching token, whether
  or not anyone is signed in — the check sits in front of the filter chain, not behind
  authentication. A write without the header answers:

    403 { "message": "CSRF token missing or invalid" }

  There is nothing special about this endpoint: every response carries the cookie, so
  any read would prime the variable just as well. It exists so there is one request
  that can be made before knowing anything, and it needs no session — the token lives
  in a cookie, not in the session, which is what lets the login request itself be
  protected.

  Order for a full run: csrf -> login -> whatever -> logout -> csrf.

  Login and logout do not treat the token the same way, and the difference is the one
  thing worth knowing here:

  - **login** sends two Set-Cookie headers, one clearing the old token and one issuing
    its replacement. The collection script skips the empty value, captures the new one,
    and the sequence carries on.
  - **logout** sends only the deletion. Nothing replaces it, so the script finds no
    token to capture and {{csrfToken}} keeps the value that just died. The next write
    answers 403. **Run this request again after every logout.**

  ### When re-running this request does not help

  The server only issues a token to a client that arrives without one; when the cookie
  is already there it is loaded and reused, and the response carries no Set-Cookie at
  all. The script therefore updates nothing, and a run that starts with a populated
  cookie jar and an empty {{csrfToken}} stays broken however many times this request is
  repeated.

  Bruno gets into that state on its own: the cookie jar is written to disk and survives
  a restart, while variables set with bru.setVar() do not. Restart Bruno, and the jar
  still holds a token that no variable matches.

  The fix is to make the next request arrive cookie-less: **clear this collection's
  cookies**, then run this request. Nothing else does it.

  ### Reading a 403

  The body cannot tell you which half is wrong — a missing cookie, a stale header and
  an unresolved {{csrfToken}} all answer the same "CSRF token missing or invalid", since
  RestAccessDeniedHandler answers every CsrfException alike. Bruno's Timeline tab shows
  what actually went out: you want a Cookie: XSRF-TOKEN and an X-XSRF-TOKEN header
  carrying the same value.
}
